FeelTheBeat — beat-synced desktop overlay visualizer
====================================================

Captures system audio, detects beats with per-band spectral flux onset
detection, and plays a transparent click-through animation overlay in time
with the music.  Runs on Linux and Windows.


Running
-------

Installed, it is a normal application: launch FeelTheBeat from the Start menu
(Windows) or the application menu (Linux), or run `feelthebeat` from a shell.
No launcher script, no flags.

From source:

    pip install -r requirements.txt
    python main.py

Linux captures the PulseAudio/PipeWire monitor source (auto-selected via
pactl).  Windows captures the default output device through WASAPI loopback,
so no device configuration is needed.


The tray icon
-------------

Click the tray icon for:

    ⚙  Settings…      every tunable, applied live
    ☕  Donate         ko-fi.com/zerothescyther
    🔓 Unlock         drag the overlay, then Lock again
    ⬌  Flip           face left / right
       Quit

Hovering the tray icon shows the current BPM.  Double-clicking it toggles the
lock.

Settings
--------

Everything applies immediately — drag a threshold and it takes effect on the
next audio block — but nothing is written to disk until you press Save.
Revert restores the values from when you opened the window; Reset to defaults
restores the built-in ones.

"Open Visualizer" brings up the live spectrum view, which is the point of the
live thresholds: put the two side by side and drag Heavy threshold until the
dashed red line sits just above the noise and only the snare crosses it.

Lock and Flip are the exception — like their tray counterparts they apply and
save immediately.  The input device is the other exception: it is saved on
Save but only takes effect on restart, since a different device can mean a
different sample rate and that resizes the detector's whole FFT band layout.


Saving a setup from the command line
------------------------------------

Pass your flags once with --save-config; they become the defaults and the app
can then be started with no arguments at all, including by double-click:

    feelthebeat --dual --heavy-mode adaptive --heavy-threshold -50 \
                --filter-apps youtube,spotify --save-config

    feelthebeat            # from now on, same thing

    feelthebeat --show-config     # print what is saved
    feelthebeat --reset-config    # forget it
    feelthebeat --no-dual         # override a saved --dual for one run

Flags given on the command line always beat saved ones.  Window position and
flip direction are saved automatically when you move or flip the overlay.

    python main.py --list-devices     # see what can be captured


Optional integrations
---------------------

  Now-playing BPM lookup   Linux: playerctl (MPRIS)
                           Windows: built in (WinRT media session)

  --filter-apps            Linux: pactl (pulseaudio-utils)
                           Windows: built in (pycaw / Core Audio sessions)

BPM data provided by GetSongBPM (https://getsongbpm.com).  To use it, put your
key in a .env file:

    GETSONGBPM_API_KEY=your_key_here

.env is read from the config directory first, then next to the executable,
then next to the source.  Without a key, BPM falls back to Deezer and then to
audio-only autocorrelation estimation.


Where state lives
-----------------

    Linux     ~/.config/FeelTheBeat/
    Windows   %APPDATA%\FeelTheBeat\

Holds settings.json (window position, flip state), an optional .env, and — for
the packaged Windows build launched from Explorer — feelthebeat.log.  Launching
the .exe from cmd/PowerShell prints to that terminal instead.


Icons
-----

assets/icon.png is the artwork master.  packaging/make_icon.py derives
assets/icon.ico (multi-resolution, for the Windows exe, tray and taskbar) and
assets/icon-256.png (for the Linux hicolor theme); both are generated, and only
the master is committed.  Replace icon.png and re-run it to change the icon.


Building packages
-----------------

    pip install -r requirements.txt -r requirements-build.txt
    python packaging/make_icon.py

    bash packaging/build_deb.sh                        # Linux .deb
    python -m PyInstaller --noconfirm FeelTheBeat.spec # Windows .exe bundle

PyInstaller cannot cross-compile, so the .exe must be built on Windows.
.github/workflows/build.yml builds both on every push and attaches them to a
GitHub Release on any v* tag.

Install the .deb with:

    sudo apt install ./dist/feelthebeat_<version>_amd64.deb
